virtio-fs: a queue for notifications from the device

Linux's virtio-fs driver has a high-priority queue and request queues, and
nothing else. Both carry traffic from the guest to the host, so FUSE's
reverse channel — the one a server uses to say "forget what you cached
about this file" — has nowhere to go over virtio-fs, and a share can only
be cached for a fixed timeout that nothing is able to shorten.

That timeout is the whole performance story. Measured on this machine, a
metadata walk of an installed package tree costs 275ms with a 100ms entry
timeout and 15ms with an unbounded one, against 62ms for the same walk on
the Mac's own disk. The difference is entirely dentry revalidation, and
the only reason we cannot simply raise the timeout is that we would then
have no way to correct the guest when the host changes something.

So: one more virtqueue, written by the device, carrying FUSE's own
notification messages verbatim — a fuse_out_header with `unique` of zero
and the notification code in `error`, exactly as a server writes to
/dev/fuse. The device advertises VIRTIO_FS_F_NOTIFICATION; a driver that
does not negotiate it behaves as before. The queue is placed last rather
than second so that both sides agree about every other index either way.

Only the two invalidations are handled, which are the two that matter for
a shared directory: INVAL_ENTRY for a name appearing or disappearing, and
INVAL_INODE for contents changing. Everything else is ignored rather than
rejected, so a future notification can be added on the device side without
this becoming a compatibility problem.

Notifications are consumed in a work item because invalidating a dentry
takes inode locks and may sleep, and because nothing in the guest is
waiting on the result.

